A||||16||WisconsinGT||Thermostat help
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||08-19-99||02:19 PM||||I have half my engine apart. while I am waiting for new heads to arrive, I would like to know what I can put into my 88GT. Can I put a 180 degree thermostat? Is that all I need? Thanks! 
Z||000001||theogre||08-19-99||02:28 PM||||a 180F thermostat is fine with the ECM. If you go colder you'll need to chip the ECM. You can also use the lower temp fan swich with the 180 stat and the ECM will still be happy. 
Z||000002||WisconsinGT||08-19-99||02:41 PM||||Thanks ogre! So, I can put the 180 stat in. Will it trip the fan at 180? Do I need both the stat and the swich? <p>You know, I found out that both heads were cracked. I am assuming it was heat related. So I am hoping that a little preventive medicine in a stat, will help in making sure this does not happen for years to come. 
Z||000003||Formula||08-19-99||04:36 PM||||If you use a chip do you need the cooler thermostat and high octane fuel
Z||000004||theogre||08-19-99||07:27 PM||||The thermosat has nothing to do with the fan. the fan is controlled by it's own temp switch. The ECM has nothing to do with the fan either. The ECM uses yet other temp sensors.<p>Using a 180F stat usually gives a block temp of around 190. By comparison, a 195 usually gives a block temp around 210-215. (Fan locked ON. {This generally holds on every cast iron motor I've tested to date, even the v8's})<p>The ECM generaly will operate properly with a <b>block</b> temp of 180 to 230f+. Above 220 you will often start to ping even tho the stock fan switch is set for 235, it depends on a few things just where the pinging will start. It could start lower or higher depending on fuel, air quality, and engine load.<p>If you use some of the aftermarket chips, you must do some or all of the following, use 90+ octain gas, install a 160F thermostat, install a low temp fan switch, remove the catalyst. For this and a number of other reasons, I generally don't think most chips should be put in street cars, especially with old motors. Some people swear by them......... 
Z||000005||cscougar||08-20-99||12:58 PM||||Where is a good nationwide auto supply in which to buy a 180 degreee thermostat?
Z||000006||DJRice||08-20-99||01:39 PM||||Pep Boys, Discount Auto Parts, Autozone....I would think that it wouldnt be that hard to find.
Z||000007||terryk||08-20-99||07:41 PM||||I personally wouldn't go below a 180F. The fuel economy will turn to s%^&.
Z||000008||Stroker||08-20-99||08:39 PM||||Advance Autoparts has the 180 thermostat here for $5.49. It is Stant part #13818. Any place that sells Stant products should have it in stock or be able to order it.<br>  Hope that helps,  <br>                    Stroker<p>
Z||000009||theogre||08-21-99||03:02 PM||||I like advance..... they are also the best place to get new rear hub units. The new ones are a bit meatier than the stock one BTW....<p>I paid 9 for my 180 at pep boys(a Stant). I like advance but there is only on in DE, and it's kind of out of my way so i tend to only go for bigger things. (there's on in MD, but then I have to pay sales tax..)
Z||000010||lowCG||08-21-99||07:19 PM||||Sounds kind of anal,but it is worth testing a new thermostat,if you've got a thermometer,just to eliminate the possibility of a sticking,or late opening stat.In hot weather,a lazy thermostat will allow a car to heat up,then it will be a while before it cools to normal temp.<br>
Z||000011||theogre||08-21-99||09:34 PM||||It's an idea, but it's hard to say when it should open fully. Engine heating time varies a bit from engine to engine. Plus with the design of the Fiero system, you can't run it with the cap off like you can with a top tank radiator and just watch for flow.<p>A non invasive test might not be a bad idea. You'd have to use a thermocouple or an IR pyrometer on the thermostat neck. The temp should stablize around a few degrees above the set value of the stat. It should do that reasonably quickly. Within about 5-7 minutes. Of course you have to do this at idle so you also have to lock the main fan on durring the test other wise the temp will keep climbing falsly.
Z||000012||lowCG||08-22-99||11:55 PM||||Actually I use a double-boiler on my stove;a fast opener,smooth/no slow spots is usually good.
Z||000013||WisconsinGT||08-23-99||07:39 PM||||Thanks all for your help. Before I go out and get a 180 stat, am I correct in thinking that my gas milage will suffer a bit putting in anything short of the stock 195 stat? Thanks
Z||000014||theogre||08-23-99||08:18 PM||||no. A 180 stat will still run the motor at about 190F, well within the range for "Closed loop" mode. In this mode the ECM will use the O2 sensor to fine match the fuel to the incoming air. ((Didn't I say this already?)) The STOCK ECM PROM is perfectly happy to run with a 180F stat.<p><br>I've been running a 180 stat for some time now. I've seen NO loss of fuel milage (I get 32 highway 27 city). I have seen that I can now use 89 octain gas and not ping any more.<p>The problem is when you run outside the Closed loop, like a 160 stat does to a stock ECM. In this mode the ECM cannot use the O2 sensor to fine tweek the fuel mix, and tends to run rich dumping fuel out the exhaust.<p>It's less than a 10$ off the sheff part. Just try it. If you don't like it it's allot cheaper than other things you could mess up spending money on. It <b>can't</b> do any damage to try it. It doesn't require any other mods to check it out.
Z||000015||Richard Parnell||09-01-99||06:23 PM||||I just plain got rid of my thermostat 2 years ago and the car runs great.
Z||000016||Patrick||09-01-99||06:28 PM||||<br>That may be Richard, but we don't all live in Hawaii.<p>Try doing that in a more temperate climate and you'd just be looking for trouble.<p>
